WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
Mental health filled the hole that religion used to occupy.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:08.480
And I say that as a physician assistant who spent 11 years working in the mental health field.

3
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.320
I've seen it happen from the inside.

4
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.520
The field is full of diagnoses and classifications, but very short on solutions for any of it.

5
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.040
It's become almost completely engulfed by the reification fallacy.

6
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:25.840
Once you see it, then you realize how much of mental health is built on top of it.

7
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.720
Take something that's defined by symptoms, time intervals, and then clinicians' judgment

8
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.800
that it's causing a problem, and then we turn it into something objective, and then we make

9
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.640
it cause other things.

10
00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.320
This is similar to sepsis syndrome.

11
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.120
So we have criteria for sepsis.

12
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:47.920
It's a group of symptoms that follow infection, but a lot of different organisms produce sepsis.

13
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:53.120
But I've never heard any physicians say that the sepsis caused the person's fever.

14
00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.480
You can't turn it into a cause of its own evidence, but psychiatry does that constantly.

15
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.520
Here are some advertisements aimed at clinicians for continuing education.

16
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:08.760
And this one says autism can hide beneath trauma, and trauma can hide beneath autism.

17
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.640
Then it says, “When ADHD looks like anxiety.”

18
00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:16.240
And here's another one that says disorganized attachment is one of the most frequently missed

19
00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.160
explanations for cases that look treatment-resistant.

20
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:22.480
They're all selling a hidden cause, but none of these things are causes.

21
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:26.280
And it goes all the way up to even the highest level of training.

22
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.680
The field has no unified definitions.

23
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.040
All these different people are defining different things in different manners, and there's no

24
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.600
official standard for most of it.

25
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.000
Take trauma, for example.

26
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.200
Trauma in the DSM—PTSD Criterion A—still requires exposure to death, serious injury, or sexual

27
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.200
violence.

28
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.600
But then if you look at the SAMHSA webpage, the federal agency for mental health, they

29
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.200
define it as anything experienced by a person as harmful.

30
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.480
Trauma is any negative experience, then it's not something pathological.

31
00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.960
It can expand to justify anything.

32
00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.560
We tore down religion, but people didn't stop needing it.

33
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.720
So it got rebuilt in therapy-speak.

34
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.520
Trauma is the original sin.

35
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.720
Therapy is the mass.

36
00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:16.000
And your diagnosis is basically the catechism that explains your fall.

37
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.840
Healing is the new salvation.

38
00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.600
And if you measure any person against perfection, everyone qualifies.

39
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.640
Suffering is real, but this field has taken the human longing for redemption and turned

40
00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.880
it into a medical specialty.
